<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leonidas I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 480 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Leonidas of Sparta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leonidas I was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> of the Agiad line who commanded the Greek force defending the pass of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> against the Persian invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> in 480 BCE. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> outflanked the pass, he sent most of the allies away and fell fighting with his 300 Spartans and their remaining allies. His stand became the classic example of Spartan courage and sacrifice.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leonidas-i.jpg" alt="Leonidas I" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: de:Benutzer:Ticinese · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helmed_Hoplite_Sparta.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
